"A composite structure diagram describes the internal roles and connections of a structured classifier.\n\nUse when: Explain collaboration between parts inside a component or class. Review delegation from an external port to an internal part.\n\nAvoid when: Only external component dependencies need explanation.\n\nVisual grammar: An outer classifier boundary encloses named, typed part roles. Ports mark interaction points on classifier or part boundaries. Connectors express assembly or delegation between roles and ports.\n\nBuild: Choose the structured classifier whose internals matter. List part roles, types, and relevant multiplicities. Place public ports on the boundary and delegate them to internal parts. Leave space between parts and boundary ports so connectors do not cross role labels.\n\nCheck: A part role is distinguished from a reusable class definition. Delegation and assembly relationships have compatible endpoints. Multiplicity describes the modeled internal configuration. Boundary titles and port names remain outside connector routes.\n\nPitfalls: Substituting arbitrary boxes for explicitly typed part roles. Mistaking a port on the boundary for a separate service.\n\nExample: A Router classifier contains parser and dispatcher parts; its input port delegates messages to the parser.\n\nAlternatives: Component diagram, Object diagram."
